IQL and the callbacks of the Gui
================================

Goal
====

IQL asks the user for structured input: a dialog with named fields, each with a kind, a prompt, a default and constraints.
IQL is a capability of System of its own: a program uses it with or without a Gui.
An IQL dialog is a window built with the widgets of the Gui library, never a dialog of the toolkit.
The IQL syntax is fluent, like the syntax of the Gui, and simpler and less flexible on purpose.
The result of an IQL query is something the program waits on.
One API waits on a result: the end of a process, the next input file, the result of a dialog.
A Frame can be disabled: while a dialog is open, the frame behind it takes no input.

Open: the IQL syntax, and the types Query and Answer.

Terms
=====

    the program        the Fearless code of one process.
    the library        the standard library code that implements CapGui, CapIql, Later and the objects they give.
    toolkit thread     the thread that receives the input of the window, lays out and paints it.
    model queue        the queue of tasks of one frame, run one at a time, in order, by the model thread of the frame.
    model task         a task of the model queue.
    callback           code of the program that the library runs when the user acts or time passes.
    model event        a model task that runs a callback.
    library task       a model task that runs only code of the library.

Threads
=======

One thread at a time runs mut code of the program.
The thread that calls CapGui.run runs the consumer of .run, then waits until the window closes.
Meanwhile the model thread of the frame runs the model events.
A model event that calls CapGui.run waits in the same way until the new window closes.
So the threads running mut code of the program form a stack, and only the top of the stack runs.
The toolkit thread runs only imm code of the program: the painters.

The getters
===========

Every getter of an object reached from CapGui is mut: the frame, the widgets, the menus and their builders.
What the user or time changes (the text of a TextField, the positions of the dividers of a Split, Frame.elapsed) is then observed only through a mut reference, as every other observation of the world.
A getter whose name is the name of a setter with no parameter takes another name: .fills is the getter of .fill.
The values the library gives to painters and callbacks keep read getters: Graphics, CaretGraphics, TextRoom, ScreenContext, ResizeRequest, TextEvent, UTextEvent, KeyEvent.
Each of them describes one moment and never changes.

The callbacks
=============

Model events, each registered by the setter on the left:

    Button.action              a click of the primary button on the Button.
    Mouse.pressed .released .moved .dragged
                               the gesture on the deepest widget with a handler for it; the gesture goes up the widget tree to it.
    Mouse.clicked .doubleClicked
                               a click: the press and the release in the same widget.
    Mouse.entered .exited      the pointer entering or leaving the widget, also when a layout or a closing menu moves the widget under the pointer.
    Widget.contextMenu         a click of the secondary button: the scope builds the menu, with its submenus.
    MenuItem.action            choosing an item without a submenu.
    Sections.onTitleClick      a click on the title of a section.
    Sections.onClose           a click on the x of a section.
    Frame.onKey                the .pressed and .released actions of a key whose first press reaches Frame.onKey,
                               and the .released actions the library runs when the focus moves, the window loses the keyboard or a new .onKey replaces the Keys.
    Frame.onClose              the close key, the close button of the desk border, Cmd+Q on macOS.
    Frame.onResize             a ResizeRequest.
    ModelFps.action            a model tick.
    _StrEditText.cleaner, _UStrEditText.cleaner, .onEdit
                               one change of the text by the user: a typed text, a text of an input method, a paste, a cut,
                               Backspace, Delete, Shift+Enter, an undo, a redo.
                               The cleaner maker makes the cleaner of the change; .onEdit runs when the text changed.
    .onCommit                  Enter, Ctrl+Enter, and the focus leaving the widget by a press elsewhere, Tab or Shift+Tab,
                               when the text differs from the committed text.

Library tasks run no callback: a press or a drag that moves the caret, the steps of the scroll of a drag past an edge, the keys that move the caret, Ctrl+A, Ctrl+C.

The toolkit thread alone handles the rest, and runs no callback:
hover highlighting, tooltips, the wheel, scroll bars, the drag of a divider of a Split,
a menu that is open (highlighting, keys, scrolling, closing, opening a submenu),
the focus, except the .onCommit and the .released actions that a move of the focus causes, the composition of an input method, the full screen key, the states of the window, the drag of the Fearless corner, the repaints.
A file dropped on the window goes to the list of the input files (CapInputCursor), with no model task.

Code of the program that runs outside callbacks:

    the consumer of CapGui.run      on the thread that calls .run, before the window opens.
    the scope of each builder       on the thread that calls the builder.
    Widget.openMenu                 the scope builds the menu, with its submenus, on the thread that calls .openMenu.
    Painter, CaretPainter           imm, on the toolkit thread, at every repaint.

Disabling a frame
=================

    Frame:{ ...
      mut .enabled(Bool): mut Frame;
      mut .enabled: Bool;
      }
    ScreenContext:{ ... read .enabled: Bool; }

A frame starts enabled.
While a frame is disabled, the library drops each model event of the frame instead of queuing it: no callback of the frame starts.
.enabled(False) also drops the model events of the frame already in its model queue.
A ResizeRequest of a disabled frame is dropped like every other model event: the bands of a desk resize made while the frame is disabled stay until the next desk resize.
The toolkit thread goes on as for an enabled frame.
Each painter sees whether the frame is enabled through .enabled of its Graphics, and can draw a disabled look.

Open: a pair cut by .enabled(False): a key pressed before it and released after it, an .entered with no .exited, a press with no release.
Open: the library tasks of a disabled frame.
Open: whether the widgets of the library draw a disabled look of their own.

Waiting
=======

    later      an object of type Later: the result of one source, there now or in the future.
    source     what gives the result of a later: the end of a process, the next input file, the answer of a dialog.
    result     what a source gives: a value, or an error.

    Later: Sealed{}
    Later[T:**]: Later{
      mut .await: T;
      mut .awaitUpTo(time: Duration): mut Opt[T];
      }
    Laters: Sealed{
      .first[R:**](laters: mut List[mut Later], cases: mut List[mut MF[R]]): R;
      .firstUpTo[R:**](time: Duration, laters: mut List[mut Later], cases: mut List[mut MF[R]]): mut Opt[R];
      }

A capability gives a later:

    CapRunningMain[R]:{ mut .end: mut Later[R]; mut .terminate: Void; }
    CapInputCursor:{ mut .file: mut Later[mut CapInputCursorNode]; mut .next: mut CapInputCursor; }
    CapIql:{ mut .ask(query: Query): mut Later[Answer]; }

CapProcess.run is CapProcess.start, then .end.await.
Later is Sealed: only the library makes a later.

    holds(l)      the later l holds its result
    result(l)     the result that l holds: a value or an error

    the source of l gives r      =>  holds(l) and result(l) = r, from then on
    l.await                      =   block until holds(l); return the value of result(l), or throw the error of result(l)
    l.awaitUpTo(t)               =   block until holds(l), at most t;
                                     not holds(l) => an empty Opt; otherwise the Opt of l.await
    Laters.first(ls, cs)         =   ls.size != cs.size => a deterministic error, before blocking;
                                     block until holds(ls[j]) for some j; i := the least j with holds(ls[j]);
                                     result(ls[i]) is an error => throw that error;
                                     otherwise return cs[i]#, run on the thread that calls .first
    Laters.firstUpTo(t, ls, cs)  =   Laters.first(ls, cs), blocking at most t;
                                     no j with holds(ls[j]) => an empty Opt; otherwise the Opt of cs[i]#

Each case is plain code: it reaches the value through the later it captures, whose .await returns at once.

    .let b= {build.end}
    .let f= {at.file}
    .let a= {iql.ask(SettingsQuery)}
    .let msg= {Laters.first(Lists#(b, f, a), Lists#(
      {"built: "+(b.await.str)},
      {"file: "+(f.await.label)},
      {"answered"}
      ))}

A later that lost a .first still gets its result from its source.
A wait whose laters can never get a result throws a non deterministic error.
The next input file can never arrive when no window of the program is open.
A process and an open dialog can always still give a result.
RevokableRunningMain, RevokableInputCursor and RevokableIql give a later that checks the Revocation at each .await, .awaitUpTo, .first and .firstUpTo on it.

A model event waits on a later as any code does: the model thread of the frame blocks, and the toolkit thread goes on repainting.
A frame waiting on a dialog:

    .button{:: .text "Settings" .action{Block#
      .do{Block#(f.enabled False, Void)}
      .let answer= {iql.ask(SettingsQuery).await}
      .do{Block#(f.enabled True, Void)}
      .return{model.apply answer}
      }}

```
A The program shows a frame f with a button Settings and a scroll bar. The action of Settings disables f, waits on the later of iql.ask(SettingsQuery), enables f and shows the answer in f.
B The user clicks Settings.
C An IQL dialog opens.
D The user clicks Settings again. No second IQL dialog opens, also after the first IQL dialog closes.
E The user drags the scroll bar of f. f scrolls.
F The user answers the IQL dialog. The IQL dialog closes, and f shows the answer.
G The user clicks Settings. An IQL dialog opens.
```
An IQL dialog runs no code of the program: the library alone runs its window, so the stack of the threads running mut code of the program does not grow.
A frame that stays enabled checks its laters from ModelFps.action, with .awaitUpTo(Duration#0) or Laters.firstUpTo(Duration#0, ...).

Implementation notes
====================

Each handoff between two threads running mut code of the program is a happens-before edge: a queue, or a wait for a task to end.
The library tasks are model tasks only so that the user and the program change a text widget in one order.
Later is Sealed, so Laters.first waits on all its laters at once, with no polling.
The revocable laters are made by a constructor internal to base.
